The mechanics matter as much as the thresholds. The body ratio and the penetration depth are close-conditional, so no decision exists before the breakout bar closes; the structural distance is known earlier, as soon as the range is frozen. The order is therefore placed on the open of the following bar, with the breakout bar's close used as the reference price for everything downstream. This is not a stylistic choice—it is the direct consequence of the Phase 1 finding that breakout-candle metrics are still in flight if an EA fires intra-bar.
Equally important is what the proposal deliberately excluded and why: volume because Phase 5 refuted it, candle size because body ratio dominates it, range size because its effect is tail-only, hour and weekday because they are secondary orderers rather than separators, and the prior-structure distance pair because the audit could not verify what reference level they measure against. Each exclusion carries a reason from the data rather than an opinion.
The assistant also flagged its own most attractive result as a trap. The pocket where both strong features agree—distance ≤ 50 and body ≥ 0.79—contained six successes, zero failures and one unresolved event. It was recorded as a hypothesis for validation rather than promoted into the rule, which is exactly the discipline that prevents a beautiful backtest from becoming a live disappointment.
Phase 11: Stop loss and take profit
Goal:
Define stop and target logic consistent with the frozen entry, anchored on entry-time quantities, with at least two approaches each and every parameter traceable to a finding.
The numbers that govern the stop came straight from the earlier phases. Maximum adverse excursion has a median of 0.49 R, a mean of 0.82 R and a maximum of 5.55 R. Successful breakouts retest to roughly 0.30 R; failures dig to roughly 0.82 R. Ninety percent of all breakouts get retested and 76% of those retests hold.
That last statistic immediately rules out the most obvious design. A stop placed at the range boundary would convert the normal, healthy retest that happens to three breakouts in four into a guaranteed exit. The stop has to sit beyond the healthy retest cluster but before the failing one, and the assistant proposed a corridor of roughly 0.35 to 0.5 R on that reasoning, with an ATR-scaled variant as an alternative and a retest-aware two-stage stop as an optional third.
For targets it proposed a fixed R multiple anchored on the median favorable excursion of 0.65 R, combined with a time gate exploiting the fact that winners resolve in a median of 14 bars while failures die in 6. Trailing after a held retest was offered as a third option and deferred, because the joint retest table needed to size it does not exist.
The most valuable part of this phase was its list of assumptions that go beyond the data. If the logger stamps SUCCESS at "MFE ≥ 1 R", then setting a take profit at 1 R is tautological and tells us nothing. The conditional distributions—favorable and adverse excursion split by outcome and direction—were never computed, so any exact stop point inside the corridor is an interpolation between medians rather than a measured breakpoint. And the 5.55 R adverse tail is uninsurable at a 26% success rate; capping it is a risk-acceptance decision, not an empirical optimum. Naming those limits is what separates an evidence-led design from one that merely quotes numbers.
Phase 12: Stress-testing before implementation
Goal:
Attack the combined entry, stop and target logic with the known weaknesses of the dataset, and find every condition under which it would knowingly take a bad trade.
Output:
Item
Verdict
Action taken
Entry structural gates
Consiste
