Keep unchanged
Stop at 0.35–0.5 R
Contradicts median MAE 0.49 and the 76% retest-hold rate
Widen the base; drop 0.35 entirely
Hard time gate at 8–10 bars
Contradicts 14-bar winner latency
Demote to a soft trail plus a session-end exit
Symmetric management across directions
Inconsistent with DOWN's deeper MAE and shorter MFE
Make management direction-conditioned
BrkDistATR confirmation, applied symmetrically
Direction-biased and possibly misleading
Suspend pending per-direction validation
Late breakout at 7–14 bars passing the gates
Known bad trade, currently admissible
Reintroduce timing as a soft preference
The stop finding is the sharpest. A 0.35–0.5 R stop sits near the population median MAE, so roughly half of all events reach it, including many eventual winners. Such a stop does not protect against the 5.55 R tail in any meaningful sense; it simply exits early, and it turns the 76% retest-hold statistic against us by selling at the worst available price. No single static stop can satisfy both the 0.49 R median and the 5.55 R tail. The design must choose which risk to prioritize. The wide stop was chosen as the base, with the failure mode handled instead by a dead-man exit when price closes back inside the range—the behavioral signature of a FALSE event. The wide stop does not cover the tail either: at 0.60 R it caps the loss well short of 5.55 R. It buys room for the healthy retest, and the tail is accepted risk rather than insured risk.
The timing finding is the one that changed the entry. Nothing in the Phase 10 logic prevents a trade firing 7 to 14 bars after the range ends, and that zone showed 60% failures with the worst follow-through in the dataset. The structural gates and the timing axis are not correlated, so there is no reason to believe one rescues the other. Timing came back in—but as a size multiplier rather than a filter, which is what Phase 9's evidence actually supports.
