The input list is unusual for a breakout EA in what it does not contain. There is no volume filter, no minimum candle size, no minimum or maximum range width, no ATR regime filter and no day-of-week switch. Those absences are not oversights; each one is a finding from Part 1 made executable. An EA that offers a volume filter invites the user to turn it on, and every hour spent tuning a parameter the data has already declared inert is an hour spent drifting away from the strategy.
The suspended confirmation deserves its own group precisely because it is switched off. Phase 12 found that penetration depth fires more readily on DOWN breakouts, which are the direction that fails two and a half times as often, making the confirmation least informative exactly where it is most needed. Rather than deleting it we left it in place, disabled, so it can be validated per direction later without any code being rewritten. A suspended feature that is documented as suspended is more honest than one quietly removed. Its group name and comments say plainly what it is: an experimental filter whose veto rejects trades outright. That is not the preference weight Phase 10 described, and the filter is off in every result reported in this article.
