The stop and target parameters are separated by direction because Phase 8 measured two different animals. UP breakouts drift: favorable excursion runs to a median of 0.78 R, adverse excursion stays at 0.46 R, and retests hold 89% of the time. DOWN breakouts fight: favorable excursion reaches only 0.54 R, adverse excursion runs to 0.70 R, and barely half the retests hold. A single symmetric configuration would have to compromise between the two and would serve neither. The values shown reflect that asymmetry directly—the UP side takes a tighter stop against a larger target, while the DOWN side needs room to breathe against a target it can realistically reach.
The body gate is implemented at 0.80 rather than the 0.79 quoted in Phase 10; Phase 7 showed the two thresholds perform identically, so the change is cosmetic, but it is a change. And the specific pair-0.60 and 0.85 for UP, and 1.50 and 0.30 for DOWN—are management choices informed by the asymmetry, not levels the dataset measured. The DOWN pair in particular needs a high strike rate to pay for itself, and the 0.60 risk multiplier reduces the size of that bet without changing its sign.
The floors are the one place where the numbers do not come from the dataset. A stop must clear the spread and the broker's minimum stop distance regardless of what the research says, so the ATR floor and spread multiple are engineering constraints. Marking them as such in the comments keeps the distinction visible to anyone reading the code later, including ourselves.
