The structures carry the decision across time. 'PendingEntry' exists because the decision and the execution happen on different bars: everything is computed when the breakout bar closes, then held until the next bar opens. Storing the fully formed stop and target rather than re-computing them at execution time guarantees that the trade we place is the trade we evaluated, even if the market gaps overnight or volatility shifts between the two bars.
'TradeState' keeps the research context alive for the duration of the position. The boundary, the range width and the ATR all belong to a range that will be overwritten when the next session begins, but the dead-man exit and the trail both need them. Copying the values into the trade state rather than reading the live globals is the same discipline that made the logger's frozen range copy necessary in Part 1, applied to a live position instead of a logged event.
