The two levels are anchored on different references, and the difference is not arbitrary. The stop hangs from the boundary, because everything the data says about adverse movement—the 0.30 R healthy retest, the 0.82 R failing retest—is measured relative to the broken level. Anchoring the stop there means a breakout that closes barely past the line automatically gets a tighter effective leash than one that closes a full ATR beyond it, which is correct: the second trade has already used up some of its room.
The target hangs from the breakout price, because favorable excursion in the dataset was measured from exactly that point. Anchoring the target on the boundary instead would shift every target by the penetration distance and quietly break the correspondence with the MFE distribution that justified the R multiple in the first place. Both levels are expressed in R—the range width—because that is the unit the logger normalized everything to. R always means range width in this article, never risk: a stop of 1.50 R is one and half range widths away, not one and half units of risk.
