Sizing is where three separate pieces of evidence converge into one number. The 'sizeMult' argument arrives already carrying the timing preference from Phase 9, the DOWN risk multiplier from Phase 8, and the optional confirmation upgrade from Phase 10. Rather than having each of those conditions reach into the lot calculation independently, they are composed into a single multiplier upstream in 'EvaluateEntry', which keeps this function doing one job and makes the composition auditable in the journal line that prints the final multiplier.
Computing the lot from the actual stop distance rather than a fixed pip value matters more here than in a typical EA, because our stop is a proportion of the range and the range varies from 97 to 1,192 points across the dataset. A fixed-distance assumption would mean risking twelve times more on a wide-range day than on a narrow one. Deriving the loss per lot from 'SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE' and 'SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE' keeps the risk constant in currency terms regardless of which kind of session we are trading.
The broker's minimum lot is the one place where that guarantee can fail. On a wide range, or when the timing and DOWN multipliers stack to a small size, the risk-based lot can fall below the minimum. Raising it to the minimum then risks more than the planned percentage. 'InpSkipBelowMinLot' decides what happens. Left at false, the default used in every reported test, the EA trades the minimum lot and writes the actual and planned risk to the journal. Set to true, it skips the trade instead. Either way the end-of-run summary counts how often it happened, so the excess risk is never silent.
