Placing the order one bar after the decision is the honest implementation of a bar-close trigger, and it introduces a risk that has to be handled explicitly. Between the breakout close and the next open, price may move so far that the planned stop becomes invalid. Entering anyway would produce a trade with a negative expected outcome before it starts, so the sanity check abandons the entry instead. This is a small piece of code that exists purely because we chose to be accurate about when information becomes available rather than convenient.
The target gets the same treatment. A gap at the open can carry price to the target, or so close to it that the broker would refuse the order under its minimum stop distance. Opening anyway would either fail or create a trade with almost no reward left. So the EA treats the signal as missed and drops it, exactly as it does for the stop. It does not recalculate the target or open without one, because either would be a trade the research never described. The summary line counts these cases as 'target already reached'.
Note also that the stop distance used for sizing is recomputed from the actual execution price, while the stop level itself stays exactly where the research put it. The trade is placed at whatever price the market offers, but the risk is measured against the real distance to the real stop, so a slightly unfavorable open reduces the position size rather than silently increasing the risk. One consequence follows. The target stays where the research put it while the fill can be better or worse than the breakout close, so the realized reward-to-risk drifts slightly from the planned one every trade.
