The order of these four checks is the design. The dead-man exit runs first because closing back inside the range is the defining behavior of a FALSE event, and Phase 12 assigned it the job that the tight stop was originally meant to do. The wide stop exists to survive a healthy retest rather than to insure the tail, so this is what handles the original failure: the position closes on evidence of the failure pattern rather than on a price level that half of all events reach anyway. The 0.25 R depth is a chosen level, not a measured breakpoint.
The session-end exit is the closest live analogue of something that only existed as a data artifact. The two are not equivalent: censoring ends an observation, while a forced close realizes a profit or a loss. In the logger, events that ran past the tracking horizon were marked truncated and labelled NEUTRAL—right-censored observations. A live position has no such label; it simply stays open. Closing at a fixed time is the only way to reproduce that boundary, and it is worth flagging that the dataset cannot validate the specific time chosen. It is an assumption, and the code comment says so.
The trail starting at bar 16 is a direct correction from Phase 12. The original proposal had an exit rule firing at 8 to 10 bars, which would have culled precisely the slow winners that the 14-bar median describes. Waiting until winner latency has elapsed before tightening anything means the trail protects mature profits instead of truncating developing ones. The trail also checks the broker's stop level and refuses to move the stop past the current market, which is the kind of defensive detail that turns a working idea into a working EA.
