Management runs before execution, and execution runs before the search for a new breakout. That sequence prevents the EA from opening a second position on the same bar it closes the first, and it guarantees that a pending entry is either fired or discarded before anything new can overwrite it. The else branch that clears the pending flag is doing quiet but important work: if a position is somehow already open when the pending entry comes due, the entry is dropped rather than queued, because a trade that fires a bar late is no longer the trade the evidence described.
Everything runs on closed bars only. The window edge detection, the range building and the breakout search are all identical in structure to the logger from Part 1, which is deliberate. If the EA built its ranges differently from the instrument that produced the evidence, none of the thresholds would mean anything. The strongest guarantee that this Expert Advisor is trading the strategy we studied is that the code which defines the range is the same code we studied it with.
